hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1168","Muammal bin Hisham Al-Yashkari, Ismail, meaning Ibn Aliya, told us on the authority of Mansour bin Abdul Rahman, and he is Al-Ghadani who is said to have said: Al-Ashal has it, on the authority of Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, on the authority of Masruq, that he entered upon Aisha and asked her about the prayer of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, She said: “He used to pray thirteen rak’ahs at night, then he prayed eleven rak’ahs, he left two rak’ahs, then he was arrested when he was arrested.” He prayed nine rak'ahs at night, and his last prayer of the night was Witr. Then perhaps he came to this bed of his, and Bilal would come to him. So he calls him to prayer.” Abu Bakr said: We take all the reports that we reported in the book of Al-Kabir, regarding the number of prayers of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, at night. The difference of narrators in their number is similar to their difference in these reports that I mentioned in this book. The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, used to pray in Some nights he prayed more than others, so everyone who reported from the Companions of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, or from his wives, or others from Women, that the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, prayed a number of prayers during the night, or he prayed in a specific manner. The Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, prayed that prayer on some occasions. Nights, with that number, and with that characteristic, and this difference from the type of permissibility, it is permissible for a person to pray any number of prayers he likes, including It was narrated on the authority of the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, that he prayed them, and according to the description that was narrated on the authority of the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, that he prayed them, there is no prohibition on anyone from Some of it"
